ccess","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"The answer covers the central stages accurately: the application with its legal grounds and supporting memo, the notice with its hearing-request period, and the order issued if no hearing is requested. It also avoids giving a universal total approval time and correctly says the 45-day period is not a guaranteed approval. The Rule 0-5 content has substantive omissions and errors. Eligibility is described only loosely as 'substantially identical to relief already granted'. It omits the requirement of two precedent applications granted within three years before initial filing, with the same relief and conditions and only immaterial factual differences. It also omits the prominent request, the comparison markups and the signed explanatory certification. It mischaracterizes the clock as a period 'for Commission action on the notice'. The key point is that within 45 days the SEC issues either a notice or an ineligibility/additional-time response. It omits the clock pauses: modification requests pause it until day 14 after a compliant amendment, unsolicited amendments until day 30, and irregular SEC office closures also pause it. It omits deemed withdrawal when no response amendment is filed within 30 days. It does not first ask whether Rule 6c-11 already supplies the needed relief. Its claim that the Commission decides whether to accept expedited review is vague rather than tied to the eligibility test. The answer is also truncated at the end.\"}","ttft_ms":4477,"type":"result","duration_ms":4492,"uuid":"258da9f5-2f7f-4865-af5c-886a0bc65d15","ttft_stream_ms":553,"time_to_request_ms":73,"first_content_frame_ms":553,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
